 harness property), the J0c workers/cycles/sanitize legs, `bb test:jolt-j5`, both platforms (J5 findings `1793098786624`); the limits that survive are the next row. Platform qualification (owner waiver \"linux only\"): the corrected crash-depth driver, the -4 parity/sanitizer legs and the shell corpus of that closure ran on Linux x86_64 only (shiva, jupiter); the earlier macOS arm64 results predate those fixes. REOPENED 2026-10-28: the codex review of e4d43e5e found four prescan bypasses (a CR/form-feed-ended comment and backslash-space joins the prescan misread, uncounted comprehension `for` chains, unary runs discarded inside brackets, and flat binary chains whose deep trees the recursive walkers overflowed), and the Architect ruling replaced the prescan rather than repairing it (slice J5b). Closing evidence 2026-10-28 in J5b; the row closes with J5 when the codex re-review of J5b signs off (pending): the prescan is deleted; guards inside recognition refuse as status -7 `nesting <m> <b> rule|lookahead` before any buffer exists: 8,192 live rule frames (the frame depth is the length of the current context's parent chain, which equals the live rule activations; recorded per context at virtual rule entry), 4,096 SLL lookahead links and 256 full-LL links past the outer context (on every RULE transition of prediction); both walkers are iterative; recognition, export and worker disposal run on a wrapper-owned 16 MiB thread (spec §7.6). Evidence: `bb probe:jolt-j5` on macOS arm64 (the review's repros refusing three times then parsing `x * 6`; at/over per shape exactly at the predicted n; 10x the old crash depths; the old crash depths in children on the jolt thread; a 20,000-deep `1+` tree equal to the JVM's packet), `bb calibrate:jolt-j5` (every bound at most a quarter of the parse stack per shape, worst 1.6 MB), the lifecycle and accessor legs; Linux x86_64 (shiva 2026-10-29), all at the round-3b bytes: `bb probe:jolt-j5` rc 0, `bb test:jolt-j5` ALL PASS 0 FAIL, `bb calibrate:jolt-j5` rc 0 (worst quarter-stack margin unchanged), the J0c legs rc 0 (cycles 20,000 with 16-byte malloc growth, workers, parity zero pins), the sanitizer soak's 50 rounds all pass (its exit-time LeakSanitizer report is the classified P3 row below — suppressed, the corpus reads HARNESS ok), and the leak experiment is cycle-independent (0/5/50/100 cycles all exactly 181,088 bytes in 3,312 allocations) | P2 |\n| jolt Python parser limits surviving J5: no process crash isolation (an uncatchable native fault ends the jolt process); no worker-restart claim; no cancellation of an under-bound parse (time is bounded only through the input and nesting axes); TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails; an above-bound source the JVM parses is refused on jolt (a recorded divergence, not a parity pin). J5b adds: the lookahead bounds (4,096 SLL links, 256 full-LL links past the outer context) refuse nesting the JVM parses (nested calls past 408 levels, bracket nests past 453 inside a call, unary runs past 253); time inside the bounds is not bounded by them: the pinned runtime parses a flat left-recursive chain in quadratic time (`1+1+...`: 16,000 operators 7.4 s, 100,000 about 340 s natively on macOS arm64; a 4 MiB chain hours) and an SLL decision whose lookahead walks a `*` chain grows faster (`1*1*...`: 2,000 operators 21 s, 4,000 about 490 s, refused at 4,097 links), so an under-bound source can hold the parse thread for minutes; the guards add up to about 2x on prediction-heavy shapes; a worker freed when no parse thread can be started is leaked, never destroyed on the caller's stack; the JVM oracle's own exporter overflows its default stack on a 20,000-deep tree (the comparison runs it with `-Xss1g`); a prediction whose context graph exceeds 4x the lookahead bound in distinct nodes refuses (a cap, not an undercount); measured, not gated: the backslash-joined chained